        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 816,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the weighted average of an array: each value counts in proportion to its weight. Without weights it is the same as np.mean.\n\nWeights let more trustworthy, more recent or more important values pull harder: a pose averaged over several sensors weighted by each one's confidence, a position blended between targets by how near each is, the centre of mass of joints weighted by the mass of each limb, a smoothing window that favours the latest frame.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.average <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values (a list is turned into one). Every input is averaged and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nHow much each value counts, held: send them first. Either one weight per element along the axis (one per row, for axis 0), or the same shape as the whole array. They need not add up to 1 - np.average divides by their total - but they must not all be 0. Without weights every value counts the same.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone averages everything to one value. A number averages along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one average per column (each row weighted), 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\naverage:\nThe weighted average.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0 and 10, weighted 3 and 1: the 0 counts three times as much, so the average is 2.5, a quarter of the way along - where np.mean gives the halfway 5. Change the weights and click them to move the average. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean is the unweighted average. lerp and crossfade blend two values by one fraction. np.robust_mean down-weights outliers automatically.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 800,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the weighted average of an array: each value counts in proportion to its weight. Without weights it is the same as np.mean.\n\nWeights let more trustworthy, more recent or more important values pull harder: a pose averaged over several sensors weighted by each one's confidence, a position blended between targets by how near each is, the centre of mass of joints weighted by the mass of each limb, a smoothing window that favours the latest frame.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.average <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values (a list is turned into one). Every input is averaged and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nHow much each value counts, held: send them first. Either one weight per element along the axis (one per row, for axis 0), or the same shape as the whole array. They need not add up to 1 - np.average divides by their total - but they must not all be 0. Without weights every value counts the same.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone averages everything to one value. A number averages along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one average per column (each row weighted), 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\naverage:\nThe weighted average.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0 and 10, weighted 3 and 1: the 0 counts three times as much, so the average is 2.5, a quarter of the way along - where np.mean gives the halfway 5. Change the weights and click them to move the average. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean is the unweighted average. lerp and crossfade blend two values by one fraction. np.robust_mean down-weights outliers automatically."
        },
